From 7f274443c40d5a69664d9833f1dacb3b3cc1bbb5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 22:01:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Documentation: a Sphinx manual, with the examples under
 test
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The `Documentation` URL in `pyproject.toml` pointed at the README. For a package
whose central claim is that a twin should be able to justify its own answers,
that is not enough surface to explain the argument, and the README was being
asked to be a tutorial, a reference and a manifesto at once.

Sphinx rather than MkDocs, and that is a fact about the source rather than a
preference: `forecast/conformal.py` already uses native reST roles (`:mod:`,
`:func:`, `:attr:`) while `model/phs.py` uses Google-style `Args:`. Napoleon
reads the second, Sphinx reads the first, and mkdocstrings would render
`:func:` as literal text.

**The reference section is generated, never written.** `autosummary` walks the
package and reads the docstrings that `pytest --doctest-modules` already
executes on every commit. A hand-written parameter table is a second source of
truth that goes stale the first time somebody changes a default, with nothing to
say so.

**The narrative examples are executed too.** `tests/test_docs_examples.py` runs
every ```python block in document order, one namespace per page, and compares
what it prints against the ```text block the page claims follows it. On its
first run it caught a transcribed output that was right in the shell I ran it in
and wrong in the sequence a reader meets. Illustrative fragments are marked
```{code-block} python and are not run — the distinction is visible in the
source of every page, so nobody pastes half a snippet.

Pages: install, a quickstart that goes from an energy balance to a calibrated
band to a refusal, seven concept pages carrying the equations, and one guide per
ISO 13374 block.

Four docstring defects, found by building:

- `estimate/energy.py` had a section underline one character short.
- `model/library.py` had ASCII structure diagrams indented under plain text,
  which reST reads as definition lists and block quotes.
- `model/integrators.py` had a bare `|...|` pair in the Jacobian expression,
  read as a substitution reference.
- `forecast/conformal.py` annotated a field `npt.NDArray[np.bool_]`. Rendered
  into a parameter description, the trailing underscore is a hyperlink
  reference to a target that does not exist. Named `BoolArray` instead, which
  the source is better for anyway.

The build is clean under `-W`: 234 warnings at first run, 0 now, none
suppressed. Read the Docs enforces the same through `fail_on_warning`, and CI
gains a `docs` job so a broken cross-reference fails a pull request rather than
rendering as plain text nobody notices for six months.
